[Chapter "Exchange Slav"]
[Line "Exchange: 3.cxd5"]
[Opening "Slav Defense: Exchange Variation"]

{White makes the position symmetrical. Black develops the same way and is fine.} 1. d4 d5 {Claim the centre.} 2. c4 {The Queen's Gambit: White attacks d5.} 2... c6 {The Slav: support d5 with a pawn and keep the c8 bishop's diagonal open, unlike ...e6.} 3. cxd5 {The Exchange Slav: a symmetrical, quiet structure.} 3... cxd5 {Recapture with the c-pawn.} 4. Nc3 Nf6 {Develop.} 5. Bf4 Nc6 {Pressure on d4. Copying White's development is fine in this symmetrical structure.} 6. e3 Bf5 {Bishop out before ...e6.} 7. Nf3 e6 {Close the chain behind the bishop.} 8. Be2 Bb4 {Pin the knight and get ready to castle.} *
